There's no `.csproj` yet, so I'll give you the general recommendation:

## Use `TimeProvider` (built into .NET 8+)

It's the lightest option — no DI container, no external packages. Just accept a `TimeProvider` with a default:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

### In production — nothing changes:
```csharp
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(); // uses real clock
```

### In tests — full control:
```csharp
var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fakeTime);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

`FakeTimeProvider` comes from the **`Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`** NuGet package. You can also advance time with `fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(5))`.

### Why this is the lightest:
- No interface to define yourself
- No DI container needed
- Optional parameter means zero breaking changes for callers
- Framework-supplied abstraction (not a hand-rolled one)

